WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:07.790
I found the UNet architecture useful for many applications I've worked on and this

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:13.710
is one of the most important and foundational neural network architectures of computer vision

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:14.710
today.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:19.910
So in this video, let's dig into the details of exactly how the UNet works.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:21.670
This is what the UNet looks like.

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:23.670
I know there's a lot going on in this picture.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:24.670
It's okay.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:27.950
We'll break it down and step it through one step at a time.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:33.790
But I want to quickly show you the final outcome of what a UNet architecture looks like.

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:38.310
And this, by the way, is also why it's called a UNet because when you draw it like this,

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.990
it looks a lot like a U.

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:45.390
But let's break it down and build this back up one piece at a time.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:50.110
So we're going to make sure we know what all of the things going on in this diagram actually

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:51.230
are doing.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:56.510
These ideas were due to Olaf Rauneberger, Philip Fisher and Thomas Brox.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:01.910
One fact, when they wrote their original UNet paper, they were thinking of the application

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:06.950
of biomedical image segmentation, really segmenting medical images.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:12.150
But these ideas turned out to be useful for many other computer vision semantics segmentation

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.030
applications as well.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:22.910
So the input to the UNet is an image, let's say is H by W by three for three channels,

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:24.510
RGB channels.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:30.430
I'm going to visualize this image as a thin layer like that.

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:36.830
I know that previously we had taken neural network layers and drawn them as 3D blocks like

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:43.390
this, where this might be H by W by three.

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:48.430
But in order to make the UNet diagram look simpler, I'm going to imagine looking at

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:49.430
this edge on.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:58.470
So all you see is this, and the rest of this is hidden behind this dark blue rectangle

00:01:58.470 --> 00:01:59.470
that I've drawn.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:01.430
And so that's what this is.

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:08.910
The height of this is the height H, and the width of this is three.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:13.030
The number of channels, the depth is three, so it looks very thin.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:17.790
So to simplify the UNet diagram, I'm going to use these rectangles rather than 3D

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.790
layers to illustrate the algorithm.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:26.230
Now the first part of the UNet uses normal feed-forward neural network convolutional

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:27.390
layers.

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:34.510
So I'm going to use a black arrow to denote a convolutional layer followed by a ReLU

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:36.270
activation function.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:41.470
So the next layer maybe looks like that, will increase the number of channels a little

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:46.470
bit, but the dimension is still high by width by a little bit more channels.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:51.310
And then another convolutional layer with a ReLU activation function.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:55.590
Now we're still in the first half of the neural network.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:00.010
We're going to use max pooling to reduce the height and width.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:06.390
So maybe you end up with a set of activations where the height and width is lower, but maybe

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.710
a sticker, so the number of channels is increasing.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:18.110
Then we have two more layers of normal feed-forward convolutions with a ReLU activation function.

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:22.150
And then let's apply max pooling again, and so you get that.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:26.950
And then repeat again, and so you end up with this.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:33.070
So so far, this is the normal convolution layers with activation functions that you've

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:38.590
been used to from earlier videos with occasional max pooling layers.

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:45.170
So notice that the height of this layer, height and width, is now very small.

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:51.710
So we're going to start to apply transpose convolution layers, which I'm going to denote

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:57.830
by the green arrow in order to build the dimension of this neural network back up.

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:03.430
So with the first transpose convolutional layer, or trans-conf layer, you are going

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:07.590
to get a set of activations that looks like that.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:14.150
In this example, we did not increase the height and width, but we did decrease the

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:16.130
number of channels.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:21.070
But there's one more thing you need to do to build a unit, which is to add in that skip

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:25.910
connection, which I'm going to denote with this gray arrow.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:31.510
What the skip connection does is it takes this set of activations and just copies it

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:32.910
over to the right.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:36.390
And so the set of activations you end up with is like this.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:41.750
The light blue part comes from the transpose convolution, and the dark blue part is just

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:44.790
copied over from the left.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:51.710
To keep on building up the unit, we are going to then apply a couple more layers of the

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:57.990
regular convolutions, followed by a relu activation function, so denoted by the black

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:00.550
arrows like so.

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:05.490
And then we'll apply another transpose convolution layer, so green arrow.

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:10.850
And here we're going to start to increase the dimension, increase the height and width

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:11.850
of this image.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:15.730
And so now the height is getting bigger.

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:19.010
But here too, we're going to apply a skip connection.

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:23.890
So there's a gray arrow again, we're going to take this set of activations and just

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:28.650
copy it right there over to the right.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:33.370
More convolutional layers and other transpose convolution skip connection.

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:38.090
Once again, we're going to take this set of activations and copy it over to the right.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:44.690
And then more convolutional layers, followed by another transpose convolution, skip connection,

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:46.490
copy that over.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:53.530
And now we're back to a set of activations that is the original input images height and

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:54.530
width.

00:05:54.530 --> 00:06:00.090
We're going to have a couple more layers of a normal feed forward convolutions.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:06.410
And then finally, to take this and map this to our segmentation map, we're going to use

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:13.810
a one by one convolution, which I'm going to denote with that magenta arrow to finally

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:18.930
give us this, which is going to be our output.

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:23.530
The dimensions of this output layer is going to be h by w.

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:29.970
So the same dimensions as our original input by num classes.

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:35.290
So if you have three classes to try to recognize, this will be three, if you have 10 different

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:41.890
classes to try to recognize in your segmentation map, then that last number will be 10.

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:46.890
And so what this does is for every one of your pixels, you have h by w pixels, you

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:53.170
have, you know, an array or a vector, essentially of n classes numbers that tells you for one

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:58.250
pixel, how likely is that pixel to come from each of these different classes.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:03.650
And if you take an argmax over these n classes, then that's how you classify each of the

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:05.970
pixels into one of the classes.

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:10.450
And you can visualize it like the segmentation map shown on the right.

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:11.450
So that's it.

00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:16.170
You've learned about the transpose convolution and the unit architecture.

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:18.810
Congrats on getting to the end of this week's videos.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:24.050
I hope you also enjoy working through these ideas in the program exercise.

